---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CurrentlyPill from '../components/CurrentlyPill.astro';
import { site } from '../config';

const certs = [
  { name: 'AWS Certified DevOps Engineer – Professional', year: 'May 2026' },
  { name: 'AWS Certified Advanced Networking – Specialty', year: 'Aug 2025' },
  { name: 'AWS Certified SysOps Administrator – Associate', year: 'Feb 2025' },
  { name: 'AWS Certified Solutions Architect – Associate', year: 'Mar 2024' },
  { name: 'AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner – Foundational', year: 'Nov 2021' },
];

// "At a glance" - front-loaded facts for a five-second skim. Rendered with the
// existing .skills-grid idiom.
const glance = [
  { k: 'Role', v: 'Cloud Software Engineer, John Deere · since 2019' },
  { k: 'Based', v: 'Des Moines, Iowa' },
  { k: 'Focus', v: 'AI-era SDLC: harness, context, evidence, governed execution' },
  { k: 'Currently', v: 'Evidence product in production, shared team brain, cloud agent evaluations' },
  {
    k: 'Credentials',
    v: 'AWS certifications, Foundational → Professional · B.S. Software Engineering, Iowa State (Cum Laude)',
  },
];

// Eyebrow styling reused for the two dated act labels. Inline so it wins over
// the global .content cascade without adding a new class or component.
const eyebrow =
  'font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--fg-dim); margin: 2.6em 0 0.3em;';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Cloud software engineer at John Deere making AI a first-class participant in the software development lifecycle, built on experience across API platforms, digital equipment licensing, cloud infrastructure, and developer experience."
>
  <CurrentlyPill text={site.currently} />

  <h1 class="prose-serif mb-6 text-[40px] leading-[1.1] sm:text-[44px]" style="color: var(--fg);">
    I’m Mitch, making <em style="color: var(--accent);">AI</em> a first-class participant in how we
    ship software.
  </h1>

  <div class="skills-grid" style="margin-top: 1.6rem;">
    {
      glance.map((row) => (
        <div>
          <span class="skills-cat">{row.k}</span>
          <span class="skills-list">{row.v}</span>
        </div>
      ))
    }
    <div>
      <span class="skills-cat">Links</span>
      <span class="skills-list">
        <a href={site.github} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" style="color: var(--accent); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px;">GitHub</a>
        <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);"> · </span>
        <a href={site.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" style="color: var(--accent); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px;">LinkedIn</a>
        <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);"> · </span>
        <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`} style="color: var(--accent); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px;">Email</a>
      </span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="content">
    <p style={eyebrow}>Recent work · October 2025 – October 2026</p>
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">The AI-Era SDLC</h2>

    <p>
      I’m one of three engineers on a tiger team chartered by senior leadership to define how
      agentic AI changes the software development lifecycle. My focus is building and governing
      the system around the model: shared context, evidence, review, and secure execution boundaries.
      I co-facilitate an agentic engineering guild and office hours for a cohort of around 38 engineers.
    </p>

    <h3>What’s shipped</h3>
    <p>
      I helped take an <a href="/work/agentic-engineering/">engineering evidence product</a> from
      requirements to production, where it now sees steady, growing use. I wrote the requirements
      and a substantial share of the backlog, moved key views onto live data, added a personal work
      view, and shipped caching that makes those views faster while respecting data freshness.
    </p>
    <p>
      I designed and stood up the <a href="/work/team-brain/">shared team brain</a>: an
      agent-maintained knowledge repository with hybrid retrieval, a structural knowledge graph,
      and a memory lifecycle. I also wrote the team’s harness thesis and Agent Delegation Stack,
      giving engineers and leadership a vocabulary for deciding what is ready to delegate.
    </p>
    <p>
      I ran <a href="/work/secure-agent-execution/">Codex and Claude Code in cloud sandboxes</a>
      and returned inspectable patches, then implemented a separate keyless Codex pilot.
      Live verification and containment hardening for that pilot remain in progress. Alongside
      this work, I built a <a href="/work/agent-trust/">versioned trust layer</a> for skill scans
      and packaged review skills and feedback hooks that other repositories can reuse.
    </p>

    <h3>Before the tiger team · Developer Experience</h3>
    <p>
      Before joining the tiger team, I worked on the Developer Experience product
      responsible for the path from pull request to production. In late 2025 and early 2026,
      I hardened the separation-of-duties platform with Datadog observability and automatic
      incidents, consolidated a legacy processor, worked through security findings, and introduced
      agent tooling into the team’s repositories. <a href="/work/pr-to-production/">More on that work →</a>
    </p>

    <h3>The model and the harness</h3>
    <p>
      My working model is <strong>Agent = Model + Harness</strong>. The harness is the guides,
      controls, sensors, and feedback that make model capability usable in real engineering work.
      Instructions guide behavior; deterministic checks, repository policy, CI, and target-system
      permissions enforce the boundaries that have to hold. Shared context gives the agent access
      to what the team already knows, and feedback turns a repeated miss into a durable correction.
    </p>
    <p>
      The evidence product is in production. The claim that AI causes better engineering outcomes
      is still a question to test. Observational signals can show relationships; they cannot establish
      causal productivity gains. That distinction is how I turn vibes into evidence without
      pretending the evidence says more than it does.
    </p>

    <p style={eyebrow}>Earlier foundations · 2019 – 2025</p>
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">From APIs to Enterprise Platforms</h2>

    <h3>2019 – 2021 · John Deere Financial</h3>
    <p>
      I started at John Deere Financial during an early attempt to make APIs safely available to
      dealer software. The ambition resembled what a product such as Azure API Management provides:
      a common gateway for governing and exposing APIs instead of another one-off integration. I
      helped architect the platform’s <a href="/work/global-gateway/">first external-facing API</a>
      for dealer access to sales-lead data, with role-based authorization and audit logging designed
      in from the beginning. That early platform later matured into a gateway used for
      both internal and external APIs.
    </p>

    <h3>2021 – 2023 · Digital Equipment Capabilities</h3>
    <p>
      I then moved closer to the technology on John Deere machinery. On <a href="/work/license-management/">equipment licensing</a>,
      I worked behind the customer experience for purchasing digital capabilities on equipment. A secure
      data-ingestion prototype I built
      during a hackathon showed that license data could be made useful to internal teams without
      giving up isolation or access controls, and helped make the case for a dedicated enterprise
      Data Engineering team.
    </p>

    <h3>2023 – 2025 · Enterprise Cloud Foundations</h3>
    <p>
      The next chapter moved from individual products to infrastructure used across the enterprise.
      I worked on cloud foundations serving engineering teams across John Deere. I led network
      consolidation, orchestrated a DNS migration, automated
      certificate renewal, and replaced manual account and VPC deletion with an event-driven
      workflow. The work reduced operational drag and made recurring infrastructure tasks easier
      to perform consistently.
    </p>

    <p>
      The throughline across those chapters is the same instinct that now shapes my AI work: make the
      hard, important thing also the easy, default thing. Secure access that does not need to be
      reinvented. Compliance that does not depend on an engineer remembering it. Renewals that do
      not need a human in the loop. Onboarding through reproducible environments.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      I’m not interested in pulling humans out of the loop. The target is complementarity: higher
      confidence, higher quality, lower cognitive load on the person doing the work.
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      That was true when the output was a pipeline nobody had to think about. It’s just as true now
      that the output is an agent.
    </p>

    <h2>Credentials</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>B.S. Software Engineering, Iowa State University (Cum Laude, 2020). Cyber security minor.</li>
      {certs.map((c) => <li>{c.name} <span style="color: var(--fg-dim); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82em;"> · {c.year}</span></li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>Skills</h2>
    <div class="skills-grid">
      <div><span class="skills-cat">AI Engineering</span><span class="skills-list">Claude Code, Codex, Copilot, MCP, agent harness design, context engineering, subagent orchestration, knowledge curation, AI-adoption telemetry, outcome-based engineering metrics</span></div>
      <div><span class="skills-cat">Cloud &amp; IaC</span><span class="skills-list">AWS (advanced), Terraform, CloudFormation, Step Functions, Lambda, ECS Fargate</span></div>
      <div><span class="skills-cat">Networking</span><span class="skills-list">VPC architecture, Route 53 / DNS, Transit Gateway, NAT Gateway, load balancing, hybrid connectivity</span></div>
      <div><span class="skills-cat">Developer Experience</span><span class="skills-list">GitHub Actions, CI/CD shared workflows, automated dependency management (Renovate), GitHub Advanced Security, policy-as-code repository governance, internal developer portals (Backstage), Dev Containers</span></div>
      <div><span class="skills-cat">Security &amp; Compliance</span><span class="skills-list">OAuth, secrets management, certificate management, network security, compliance automation, separation of duties</span></div>
      <div><span class="skills-cat">Observability</span><span class="skills-list">CloudWatch, Datadog, Splunk, Grafana, AWS X-Ray</span></div>
      <div><span class="skills-cat">Languages</span><span class="skills-list">Python, Java, JavaScript, TypeScript</span></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Colophon</h2>
    <p>
      Built with Astro, Tailwind, and MDX; hosted on GitHub Pages. The source is public; see the
      link in the footer.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
